title: c1.1 Mindful Walk
syntax: xwiki/2.1
%%WIKI-SYNC-META%%
**Abstract.** Everything runs inside the care home, with no internet connection. Six selected: Pepper at the door (TECH-01), the pocket guide (TECH-02), landmark beacons (TECH-03), a local server with one instance of the System per resident (TECH-04), consent-gated exchange between instances (TECH-05), the walker model and starters (TECH-06). Three rejected: cloud speech and language services (TECH-07), audio recording (TECH-08), location and body sensing (TECH-09). No hands-on testing yet.

= Technology Option - Pepper at the door (selected) [TECH-01] =

**Report Format:** §1.3 Technology

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|TECH-01
|Name|Pepper at the door (selected)
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Vendor documentation; the course platform. No hands-on testing by this project.
|Category|Social Robot
|Capabilities|(((
Humanoid, about 1.2 m, wheeled base; speech, chest tablet, touch sensors.

* Before a walk: asks each walker alone, by voice with yes/no on the tablet (F1)
* Re-introduces walkers by name and photo (F2)
* Welcome back: one statement from the walk (F2)
)))
|Limitations|Flat indoor floors only. Speech recognition of older voices unreliable: answers by tablet. Humanoid form may read as supervisor. Cameras present (covered).
|Maturity and feasibility|Commercially available; supplied by the course.
|Failure and fallback behaviour|Pepper unavailable: the pocket guide asks consent in the resident's room.
|Benefits|HV-01: consent asked face to face, privately. HV-06: introductions.
|Drawbacks|Strains HV-03 (cameras). Mitigation: disabled and visibly covered.
|Selected or rejected|Selected, at the garden door only.

== Graph edges ==

enables → F1, F2; addresses → HV-01, HV-06; realized-in → RP-01 (§2.2).

= Technology Option - Pocket guide (selected) [TECH-02] =

**Report Format:** §1.3 Technology

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|TECH-02
|Name|Pocket guide (selected)
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Group reasoning. Off-the-shelf parts.
|Category|Wearable / AI-ICT
|Capabilities|(((
Palm-sized; clips to a rollator or hangs on a lanyard. Same voice as Pepper.

* Speaker and microphone; speech recognised on the device (TECH-06)
* Two large buttons: pause or answer; "going in". Pull-cord for help
* Accelerometer: walking, stopped, pace, stumble
* Beacon receiver (TECH-03)
* Phone: calls through the home's own exchange. During a call, short hints mixed into the line towards the relative only
)))
|Limitations|A speaker is public; wind; hearing aids; battery. Recognition outdoors and in dialect untested (O-3). Cannot see faces or distress.
|Maturity and feasibility|Prototype on a phone (EM-01); feasible within the project.
|Failure and fallback behaviour|Silent or flat: the walk goes on as today; staff see the state at the start.
|Benefits|HV-06: talk kept going. HV-04: stumbles and tiredness noticed. HV-03: no camera, no GPS.
|Drawbacks|Strains VT-05: it listens. Strains VT-01: help offered during a walk.
|Selected or rejected|Selected.

== Graph edges ==

enables → F1 to F5; addresses → HV-03, HV-04, HV-06; strains → VT-01, VT-05; realized-in → RP-02 (§2.2).

= Technology Option - Landmark beacons (selected) [TECH-03] =

**Report Format:** §1.3 Technology

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|TECH-03
|Name|Landmark beacons (selected)
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Group reasoning. Bluetooth beacons are standard in museum audio guides.
|Category|Sensing
|Capabilities|Small battery beacons at the pond, roses, benches and gate. The guide hears the nearest: a place for redirection (F2); the gate for an alert (F5). No positions stored.
|Limitations|Proximity only, within metres. Batteries. Garden only.
|Maturity and feasibility|High.
|Failure and fallback behaviour|Silent beacon: redirection uses the thread only; door alarms still apply.
|Benefits|Places to redirect to (HFC-01). A gate event without tracking.
|Drawbacks|A momentary location exists. Never stored; sent only inside an alert.
|Selected or rejected|Selected.

